\newlabel{tab:loo}{{3}{19}{Contribution of each PET term in IO (test set, 28 patients): network trained without PET losses (T1), IO with all PET terms or with one term removed. Mean$\pm $SD over patients. $^{\dagger }$/$^{\ddagger }$: significantly better/worse than with all terms (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on patient values, Holm-corrected within each column, $p<0.05$)}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.5}Comparison with Baselines}{19}{subsection.4.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sect:res_baselines}{{4.5}{19}{Comparison with Baselines}{subsection.4.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Comparison with baselines on the test set (28 patients, 38 follow-up$\rightarrow $baseline pairs). Values are mean$\pm $SD over patients (a patient's pairs are averaged first). Rank: challenge significance ranking, with the 95\,\% interval of the rank over 1000 patient bootstrap samples in brackets, over all methods except Initial, which is a reference (its MTV/TLG change is 0 by construction because nothing is resampled). Training / IO: PET and rigidity losses (PET + rig.) used in training and in instance optimisation (IO); IO ``--'': no IO, ``basic'': IO with the similarity and regularisation terms only. $^{\dagger }$/$^{\ddagger }$: Ours + IO with PET + rig. in training and IO (our full method) is significantly better/worse than this method (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on patient values, Holm-corrected within each column, $p<0.05$; NDV tested after the 0.005\,\% equivalence threshold). $>$ thr.: pairs with \%NDV above 0.005\,\%. Runtime per pair on one NVIDIA RTX A6000, including the affine step. Affine + IO: our instance optimisation directly after the affine pre-registration, without the network. Bold: best mean among ranked methods.}}{20}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:baselines}{{4}{20}{Comparison with baselines on the test set (28 patients, 38 follow-up$\rightarrow $baseline pairs). Values are mean$\pm $SD over patients (a patient's pairs are averaged first). Rank: challenge significance ranking, with the 95\,\% interval of the rank over 1000 patient bootstrap samples in brackets, over all methods except Initial, which is a reference (its MTV/TLG change is 0 by construction because nothing is resampled). Training / IO: PET and rigidity losses (PET + rig.) used in training and in instance optimisation (IO); IO ``--'': no IO, ``basic'': IO with the similarity and regularisation terms only. $^{\dagger }$/$^{\ddagger }$: Ours + IO with PET + rig. in training and IO (our full method) is significantly better/worse than this method (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on patient values, Holm-corrected within each column, $p<0.05$; NDV tested after the 0.005\,\% equivalence threshold). $>$ thr.: pairs with \%NDV above 0.005\,\%. Runtime per pair on one NVIDIA RTX A6000, including the affine step. Affine + IO: our instance optimisation directly after the affine pre-registration, without the network. Bold: best mean among ranked methods}{table.4}{}}
